e that racist ideas  about other countries had in this debate  now it's reasonable very logical to  understand that imperialism in its  original form and one might argue right  up to today has a strong streak of  racism in it not just American  imperialism but also the British and  French and Dutch and Portuguese and  Spanish imperialism also had this idea  that the races were ranked according to  their ability to rule and there were  lower races that were primitive and  barbaric and couldn't govern themselves  and that was why the more advanced white  races had to come in and take over their  countries and run things for them so  that was very logical argument but what  I found more interesting was that in  this debate over whether the US should  take over this big role in the world the  anti-imperialist  also had very had a very  ardan racist argument their argument was  we can't take in Cuba Puerto Rico  Arizona Philippines because we're  getting a lot of non-white people into  the US next thing you know you'll have  the senator Hawaii  the senator from Patagonia the senator  from Cuba or as a one congressman said  Mr Speaker you may even be forced to  call upon with terrifying shiver the  gentleman from the cannibal Islands the  racism played a role on both sides of  this argument and probably still that I  have one more quick question but before  I do I wanted to thank Steve for coming  and thank you ladies and gentlemen on  behalf of the printer's World Book Fair  and the Chicago Tribune and my last  question is can I get you so my book is  one worth thank you very much thank you  [Applause]
 

